\abstract[ABSTRACT]{Software-defined networks (SDN) are a common practice in enterprise IT management systems due to their simplified management and ease of use, but they also pose a latent risk: if compromised, they could compromise all services that depend on that network. Studies of this type of network typically use synthetic traffic, which undermines the value of the data because it does not accurately represent real traffic; or, alternatively, they include only a minimal number of attack vectors. This study addresses these challenges by, first, capturing real traffic at a public university using four attack vectors: TCP-SYN, HTTP, Slowloris, and ICMP, while simultaneously simulating DDoS attacks in a Dockerized environment that emulates the university’s SDN networks. Traffic classification was performed using three classification algorithms: Naive Bayes, Random Forest, and Logistic Regression. Traffic was captured in 120-second sessions for each SDN over five iterations, ultimately yielding 230,692 network packets. The results indicate that the classification methods yielded optimal results, with a minimum F1 score of 0.997 for logistic regression and 0.999 for both the Random Forest and Naive Bayes models.  The study achieves its objective by generating a multclass network traffic dataset and by establishing classification methods that can be fine-tuned using the proposed data.}

