\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Software-defined networking (SDN) decouples the control plane from the data plane, concentrating network intelligence in a logically centralized controller \citep{kreutz2015sdn}. This centralization simplifies network management, but it also turns the controller into a single point of failure: a distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attack that saturates the controller, the switches, or a hosted service can degrade or take down the entire network segment it governs. Detecting such attacks early, and separating them reliably from legitimate traffic spikes, requires datasets that are both representative of real SDN deployments and precisely labelled.

Most publicly available intrusion-detection datasets, such as NSL-KDD \citep{tavallaee2009nslkdd}, CICIDS2017 \citep{sharafaldin2018cicflowmeter}, and CICDDoS2019 \citep{sharafaldin2019cicddos2019}, were not captured inside an SDN environment: they lack OpenFlow-specific signals, such as flow-table occupancy or controller load, and are nonetheless often reused out of context to train SDN-targeted classifiers. Recent work has started to close this gap by generating DDoS datasets directly inside Mininet/Ryu testbeds. Kumar and Gupta released two single-vector datasets built this way: a TCP-SYN flood dataset \citep{kumar2025synflood} and, more recently, an ICMP-flood dataset \citep{kumar2026icmpfloodsdn} in which normal traffic is captured by connecting a Mininet host to a live internet connection and browsing real websites (YouTube, Facebook, news, etc.), instead of relying on synthetic traffic generators. This real-traffic approach increases realism, but it introduces two practical limitations for a reusable benchmark: the resulting dataset is tied to a single attack vector, and it is not reproducible across runs, since the captured content and timing depend on whatever the live internet happens to serve at capture time.

This work, Campus-DDoS-SDN, addresses both limitations within a single, fully reproducible testbed. Four attack vectors (TCP-SYN flood, HTTP flood, Slowloris, and ICMP flood) are launched from already-compromised hosts against one shared victim, a web portal, inside a Mininet/Ryu topology that mimics a university campus: a core/DMZ switch layout plus one emulated laboratory per academic area. Unlike prior single-vector or fully-separated-capture datasets, legitimate users keep browsing the portal throughout every attack, so each capture mixes benign and malicious flows against the same target, as a real DDoS incident would. Ground-truth labels are assigned per source IP rather than per capture file, and every run additionally logs the attack's effect on the victim as perceived by a real client (HTTP response code, latency, active connections), alongside network-level indicators (packets and bytes per second) and OpenFlow controller telemetry collected by a custom Ryu application (flow-table statistics, per-source flow counts and source-IP entropy sampled at a fixed interval). The complete environment, including the traffic generators, the custom web portal, and the labelling pipeline, is packaged as a Docker image to remove any dependency on a live internet connection or on a specific host operating system.

The main contributions of this work are:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\roman*.]
  \item A multi-class DDoS dataset covering four attack vectors
        against a single victim inside a realistic, multi-segment SDN topology, instead of a single attack type against a flat
        network.

  \item Per-flow, per-source-IP labelling that keeps legitimate and
        malicious traffic mixed within the same capture, closer to a real attack scenario than the fully-separated captures used in prior work.

  \item Combined network-level (flow and packet statistics via
        CICFlowMeter) and application-level (HTTP status, latency,connection saturation) impact metrics, automatically on every run.

  \item A one-command, fully reproducible Docker environment that
        does not depend on a live internet connection, addressing the reproducibility limitation of real-traffic-based SDN datasets.

  \item A random-forest classifier trained without IP addresses that
        reaches a macro F1 of \Fmacro on the held-out Ciencias Agrarias laboratory, plus a pipeline ready to mix authorized Wireshark captures of benign student traffic with simulation-only attacks.

  \item OpenFlow controller telemetry exported on every run by a custom Ryu application: it installs IP-based flows and periodically polls flow-table statistics, which the pipeline merges with the per-flow CICFlowMeter features by source IP. This supplies the SDN-specific signals that the reused non-SDN datasets lack.
  
  \item A dataset for each time window with one row per second, including portal counters and SDN telemetry, evaluated using leave-one-run-out cross-validation across 20 runs covering five intensity profiles (normal, low, stealth, pulses, and ramp). The random forest achieves macro F1 = 0{,}918~$\pm$~0{,}149 and binary F1 = 0{,}942~$\pm$~0{,}107. Ablation results show that SDN telemetry contributes $+$0{,}092 to macro F1 compared to using only network counters, confirming that OpenFlow signals complement early detection.

\end{enumerate}

High accuracy achieved using a single type of attack and separate captures does not prove that the detector can recognize malicious traffic when legitimate users continue to use the same service. Furthermore, an evaluation that relies on live web browsing varies from one run to the next. Therefore, the problem addressed is how to construct a reproducible dataset that mixes benign traffic with four attacks against the same website, and that allows the model to be tested in a lab other than those used to train it.

The question is whether flow statistics—without IP addresses, ports, or timestamps—allow us to distinguish between \texttt{BENIGN}, \texttt{synflood}, \texttt{httpflood}, \texttt{slowloris}, and \texttt{icmpflood} in an unexplored academic setting. The overall objective is to implement and evaluate this database in Docker. The specific objectives are: (i) to model a campus topology; (ii) to capture mixed traffic; (iii) to convert and tag the PCAPs; (iv) to measure the effect on the portal and the container; and (v) to evaluate a random forest with laboratory-based classification. The working hypothesis is that the characteristics of flow size, duration, and rate allow for a macro F1 score greater than 0.90 in the reserved laboratory. This hypothesis is first tested in-house; validation using real traffic from the UNCP is proposed and authorized as a subsequent phase.

\section{Materials and Methods}\label{sec:research}

  \subsection{Workflow}

  The entire experiment is launched with a single command on the Docker image \texttt{ataque-sdn}. Inside the container, the Ryu controller, Open vSwitch, and the Mininet topology are started in that order; then the requested scenario is executed, and the outputs are collected. The complete flow goes from the emulated campus to the two labeled datasets (by flow and by time window) and to their detectors (Fig.~\ref{fig:methodology}).

    \begin{center}
        \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{methodology.png}
        \captionof{figure} {Experimental pipeline from the emulated campus to the model. The label comes from the sender, not the capture file.}\label{fig:methodology}
    \end{center}

  \subsection{Topology and Population}

  The topology replicates a small campus network: an edge modem, a \emph{core} switch, a DMZ hosting the web portal (the victim, \texttt{10.0.0.10}), and one lab per academic department, connected by seven Open vSwitch switches managed by Ryu. Each lab has four students and two compromised PCs (20 students and 10 infected PCs in total). The command center (C2) is located on the Internet side of the modem (\texttt{192.168.9.10}); the entire campus resides in \texttt{10.0.0.0/8}. During an attack, students continue to request pages from the portal, and the operator’s browser connects as just another benign client (\texttt{http://127.0.0.1:8080/} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{10.9.0.1}). The attack does not leave the container.

