  \subsection{Mixed Traffic Generation}

  Compromised PCs launch one of four attack vectors: TCP-SYN flood (\texttt{hping3}), HTTP flood, Slowloris (\texttt{slowhttptest}), and ICMP flood. Sustained floods (SYN and ICMP) run in a loop with a \texttt{timeout} that restarts the generator every 5~s to maintain a consistent rate; the ICMP flood uses the kernel’s \texttt{ping}, which maintains the rate better than the \texttt{--icmp} mode of \texttt{hping3} over Open vSwitch. Since legitimate traffic never stops, each capture mixes benign and malicious flows against the same portal. The bank is orchestrated by \texttt{scripts/docker/run\_scenario.sh}, which runs one scenario or all five.

  \subsection{Capture and Measurement}

  In addition to the \texttt{pcap} captured at the portal interface, each scenario generates three synchronized data streams per second: a student probe (HTTP code, latency, and packets per second as perceived by the portal), OpenFlow controller telemetry, and container resource usage (CPU, memory, and network) via \texttt{docker stats}. The SDN telemetry is generated by a custom Ryu application (\texttt{flow\_monitor\_13.py}) which, in addition to forwarding traffic, establishes a flow for each triplet (source IP, destination IP, protocol) and periodically samples the counters for those flows on the switch (packets, bytes, and active entries). The counters are read from the switch’s data plane so that they reflect the actual volume even under sustained flooding—a necessary condition to ensure that the protocol signal (particularly ICMP) is not lost in the telemetry.

  \subsection{Tagged by Source IP}
  The fundamental truth is assigned by sender, not by capture file. A flow is \texttt{BENIGN} unless its source IP is a compromised PC and the scenario is not the \texttt{baseline}; in that case, it is named after the attack. Thus, the student, the portal, and the entire \texttt{baseline} remain \texttt{BENIGN} even within an attack capture. The compromised IP addresses are listed in the JSON file that accompanies each \texttt{pcap}.

  \subsection{Two datasets, two tasks}

  Two complementary data sets are derived from the captured traffic. \emph{By flow} (\texttt{pcap\_to\_csv.py}): TCP and UDP traffic passes through CICFlowMeter, which produces bidirectional statistics; ICMP, which does not pass through that filter, is grouped by a dedicated aggregator every 10~s (without this cutoff, each PC would leave a single, extremely long flow, and the class would end up with very few rows). Columns \texttt{sdn\_*}, derived from OpenFlow telemetry and cross-referenced by source IP, are added to each flow. Before training, the source IP, destination IP, ports, and timestamp are discarded so that the model learns behavior rather than machine identity; infinite or empty values are set to zero, and all constant columns are removed. The model is a forest of 200 trees with class-based weights, because SYN floods dominate the dataset. \emph{By time window} (\texttt{build\_windows.py}): one row per second that aggregates the portal counters and SDN telemetry for the interval (source IP entropy, traffic concentration, flow table growth, and packets per protocol), for detection \emph{while the attack is occurring}.

  \subsection(Evaluation Protocol)

  A random partition is never used, because windows and neighboring flows are correlated. The per-stream model (\texttt{analisis\_completo.py}) is evaluated using a laboratory \emph{holdout} set: four academic areas (185,127 streams) are used for training, and the model is tested on an unseen area, Agricultural Sciences (45,550 streams). The window-based detector is evaluated using \emph{leave-one-run-out} cross-validation when there are two or more runs. To obtain the mean and standard deviation, the test suite runs \texttt{run\_scenario.sh repeat N} or \texttt{dificil N}, which repeat the scenarios with different seeds and intensities (recorded in the \texttt{meta.json} file for each run). The flow-based classifier uses five 120~s runs per scenario; the window-based detector uses 20 runs that combine five intensity profiles (normal, low, stealth, pulses, and ramp), plus four never-before-seen attacks reserved solely for testing.

  \subsection{Software and Hardware requirements}

  The database runs on an Ubuntu 24.04 container; the table versions were read from the \texttt{ataque-sdn} image. The models are trained by running the analysis scripts (they are not pre-trained models): the reported detector was obtained using Python 3.10.12, scikit-learn 0.23.2, pandas 1.3.5, numpy 1.21.5, and matplotlib 3.5.1. Ryu uses Python 3.9.25 in a separate virtual environment.

