\section{Results and impact overview}\label{sec:impact}
The benchmark is fully reproducible: it is repeated using controlled seeds, mixing student and attack data in the same run, and produces two complementary detection pipelines. The flow-based classifier achieves a macro F1 score of \Fmacro in the Agricultural Sciences laboratory, retained from training. The window-based detector, evaluated using leave-one-run-out on 20 runs covering five intensity profiles, achieves a macro F1 score of 0{,}918~$\pm$~0{,}149. With institutional authorization, the same pipeline can process real benign traffic (Wireshark) without removing the attack from the container.

  \subsection{Dataset composition}

  The CSV files from the five runs total 230,692 flows: 143,133 \texttt{synflood}, 46,728 \texttt{BENIGN}, 36,595 \texttt{httpflood}, 3,598 \texttt{slowloris}, and 638 \texttt{icmpflood}. This distribution explains the decision to weight the classes during training; unlike in previous runs, the ICMP class now has enough flows (129 in the held-out dataset) to be meaningful. In addition, the attack captures retain a benign portion, corresponding to students who continue to use the portal.


  \subsection{Portal impact}
  \input{tabla_sonda_en.tex}


  SYN produces the highest rate, 466 packets/s, yet the probe keeps 120 HTTP 200 responses. HTTP flood raises mean latency from 407 to 564~ms. Slowloris produces only 86 packets/s but occupies available connections and fails 117 of 118 probes. Packet rate alone therefore does not describe application impact.

  \subsection{Classifier performance}
  \input{tabla_resultados_en.tex}

  The F1 macro in Agrarias is \Fmacro and the binary F1 for \texttt{BENIGN} against attack is 1{,}000. Among the most influential variables are both CICFlowMeter statistics (the mean and variability of the outbound packet length and the mean outbound segment size) and columns derived from OpenFlow telemetry cross-referenced by source IP (\texttt{sdn\_avg\_pkt\_bytes}, \texttt{sdn\_bytes}, \texttt{sdn\_pkts}). This is consistent with the scenarios—SYN generates small, repeated segments, HTTP exchanges complete requests and responses, and Slowloris maintains connections with low traffic—and shows that SDN signals also provide insights at the individual flow level.


  \subsection{Early Detection Based on a Time Window}

  The per-window dataset contains 11,698 one-second windows, distributed across 20 runs that combine five intensity profiles (normal, low, stealth, pulses, and ramp). BENIGN comprises 5,175 windows, and each attack vector ranges from 1,573 to 1,652 (Fig.~\ref{fig:windows_class}). To measure generalization, the dataset also reserves four never-before-seen attacks—\texttt{ackflood}, \texttt{udpflood}, \texttt{slowpost}, and \texttt{multivector}—that only appear during testing. Each scenario lasts 120~s per run.

  \begin{center}
      \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{ventanas_por_clase.png}
      \captionof{figure}{Distribution of Windows by Class in the Early Detection Dataset.}\label{fig:windows_class}
\end{center}

  Fig.~\ref{fig:packets_scenario} shows the distribution of the packet rate to the portal per second across all runs. Synflood exhibits the highest and most consistent rate. Httpflood has a lower median but with extreme outliers, reflecting the variability in intensity across runs. Slowloris is the most striking: its median is low, similar to the baseline, but it exhibits high outliers; this is consistent with its nature as a connection-exhaustion attack, rather than a volume-based attack. This dispersion confirms that packet rate alone is not sufficient to distinguish all attacks, which justifies the use of combined network, portal, and SDN telemetry features.

