  \subsection{Bank Resource Usage}

  The average CPU usage of the container was 40\% in the baseline, 36\% in SYN, 71\% in HTTP, 313\% in Slowloris, and 35\% in ICMP; in Docker, 100\% is roughly equivalent to one core. Slowloris occupied about three of the six assigned cores, while the maximum observed memory usage remained around 230~MiB (during the HTTP flood). These values measure the entire bank (Mininet, OVS, Ryu, portal, and generators), not just
  the victim.

  \subsection{Discussion}
  The test supports the hypothesis for all five classes: the model achieves a macro F1 score above 0.90 without IP and generalizes to a held-out lab. For the window-based detector, we avoid the naive interpretation of an F1 close to 1: by adding stealthy, low-intensity attack profiles—in pulses and ramps—the honest macro F1 drops to 0{,}918~$\pm$~0{,}149 (\emph{leave-one-run-out} over 20 runs). The gap between training and testing has a median per run of $-0{,}011$—the model does not memorize—and only spikes in the few runs with the stealthy profile, where the attack is nearly indistinguishable from normal traffic. The most demanding test—leaving an entire profile out of training—yields a mean macro F1 score of 0{,}742 with absolute features and 0{,}764 with relative features compared to each run’s baseline; these figures—not a value close to 1—are the defensible results. When faced with four never-before-seen attacks (\texttt{ackflood}, \texttt{udpflood}, \texttt{slowpost}, and \texttt{multivector}), the detector triggers an alarm in 97\% of the attack windows using relative features. The most informative result remains the ablation: the fact that OpenFlow telemetry contributes $+$0{,}092 to the macro F1 score and is decisive in distinguishing ICMP from SYN is concrete evidence that SDN-specific signals have discriminative value that non-SDN datasets do not offer. The difference between SYN and Slowloris also demonstrates why it is beneficial to combine flow classification with a service probe: more packets do not necessarily mean a less available page.

  \subsection{Limitations and future improvements}
  The table is still computed from emulated students. The hybrid dataset (Wireshark plus simulated attack) is ready in the code and is updated when the authorized pcaps arrive. The reported per-flow result comes from a single 120~s run; the \texttt{repeat} driver and the leave-one-run-out cross-validation now make the evaluation repeatable, and the multi-run figures (mean~$\pm$~standard deviation) are being regenerated. ICMP accounts for 120 flows in the whole set and 24 in the Agrarias test, so its perfect F1 is not reported as a settled class result. The per-window detector is designed for early detection and consumes the controller telemetry that a Ryu-side detector would use, but it still scores already-captured windows offline rather than running inside Ryu. Future work: report the multi-run cross-validated figures and the SDN ablation, repeat the test with the Wireshark background and a mixed four-vector scenario, add more ICMP samples, and move the trained per-window model into the controller for online detection.

  \section{Conclusions}
  Campus-DDoS-SDN is a reproducible, single-command testbed that does not rely on live internet connectivity and generates a multi-class DDoS dataset in which benign traffic and four attack vectors coexist in the same capture against a single portal. Source-based labeling preserves benign traffic within the attacked capture, and laboratory-based partitioning provides a more demanding test than a random split. On completed flows, a random forest trained without IPs, ports, or timestamps achieves a macro F1 score of \Fmacro in the retained Agricultural Sciences laboratory; on the window-based dataset, it enables early detection with a macro F1 score of 0{,}918 across 20 runs covering five intensity profiles (and up to 0{,}764 when leaving an entire profile out of training), where OpenFlow telemetry contributes $+$0{,}092 and proves decisive in separating ICMP from SYN. This confirms the central contribution of the study: SDN-specific signals, absent from reused non-SDN datasets, have measurable discriminatory value.

  Availability, packet rate, and classification describe different aspects of the incident: only Slowloris takes the portal offline despite its low packet rate. The result, using emulated students, serves as the basis for requesting authorization to incorporate real benign traffic from UNCP, without removing the attack from the container.

